\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Local Gaussian data and marginal restriction}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Fix a finite vertex set #{V}. For every subset #{T\subseteq V}, let
#{\mathcal G(T)} be the set of centered real Gaussian probability laws
on #{\mathbb R^T} with positive-definite covariance. Put
#{\mathcal G(\varnothing)=\{1\}}, the unit mass on the zero-dimensional
space. For #{S\subseteq T}, coordinate projection
#{\pi_{S,T}:\mathbb R^T\to\mathbb R^S} defines marginal restriction
##{\operatorname{res}_{S,T}\mu=(\pi_{S,T})_*\mu.}
If #{\mu=N(0,G)}, its restriction is #{N(0,G_{SS})}; a principal
submatrix of #{G>0} is positive definite. Identity and composition of
projections make #{\mathcal G} a [presheaf](tt-002Q) on the poset of
all subsets of #{V}.}

\p{A cover of #{T} is a finite family #{(T_i)} with
#{\bigcup_iT_i=T}. A family #{\mu_i\in\mathcal G(T_i)} is
\newvocab{compatible local Gaussian data} if its two marginals on each
#{T_i\cap T_j} agree. A global extension is a law
#{\mu\in\mathcal G(T)} with these restrictions. In covariance
coordinates, compatibility means equality of the two principal
submatrices on each overlap.}

\p{The domain contains all subsets and all such positive Gaussian laws.
It is not restricted to the Markov laws of one fixed graph: marginalizing
vertices can create new precision entries. For a law with precision
#{Q=G^{-1}}, the [Schur formula](fdip-000D) gives the precision of its
#{S}-marginal as
##{\Lambda_S=(G_{SS})^{-1}
 =Q_{SS}-Q_{S,S^c}Q_{S^c,S^c}^{-1}Q_{S^c,S},}
where #{S^c=T\setminus S}. This is generally different from
#{Q_{SS}}. Empty blocks follow the zero-dimensional convention.}

\p{The [two-singleton example](fdip-006A) shows why overlap
compatibility alone does not ensure uniqueness.}
